## Security

Findings from the full security audit (2026-06-10). Overall posture is strong — opaque HMAC-hashed session tokens (no JWT), constant-time CSRF + trusted-proxy comparison, parameterized SQL, consistent IDOR scoping, enforced deep-agent filesystem isolation, no committed keys/`.env`. The items below are the real action items, ordered by severity. Most cluster around the MCP gateway (the one unauthenticated/plaintext/privileged edge), two production credential mistakes, and inline-preview/header gaps in the UI.

### High

- Vector DB (Chroma) runs with neither TLS nor authentication — `vectordb` service in `src/docker-compose-denis.yaml` (no certs, no auth env) and `src/rag_service/main.py:83` (`chromadb.HttpClient(host, port)`, no `ssl=`; `chroma_api_impl="rest"`). The `rag_service → vectordb` hop is plaintext HTTP and Chroma 0.6.3 has no auth token, so anything on the `backend` overlay can read or write the entire vector store in cleartext (embeddings + retrieved document content). Internal-network-only (`backend` is `internal: true`) bounds the blast radius to a container on `backend` or an RCE in rag_service/agents — but it's the only data store with *neither* control (Postgres has TLS + creds; Redis now uses `rediss://` after the 2026-06-14 work). It's also the last unencrypted inter-service hop besides `agents → mcp_gateway`. Fix: enable Chroma server auth (`CHROMA_SERVER_AUTHN_PROVIDER` + `CHROMA_SERVER_AUTHN_CREDENTIALS`, client sends the token) so the store is at least authenticated like the others; for encryption, front it with a TLS-terminating sidecar (or a Chroma build with native TLS) and point `rag_service` at it over `https` with `ssl=True` + the internal CA.
- MCP gateway URL is fetched with no scheme/host allowlist, over plaintext SSE, with no internal-trust header — `src/agents/utils/mcp_tools.py:151`, `src/agents/core/settings.py:116`. It's the only inter-service hop that isn't mTLS-protected. Anyone able to influence `MCP_GATEWAY_URL` or answer DNS for `mcp_gateway` makes the agents service ingest attacker-controlled tool definitions. Fix: validate scheme/host at settings load, require `https://` + `verify=get_httpx_verify()` in prod, inject `internal_service_headers()` on the gateway connection.
- MCP tool definitions are trusted wholesale — `src/agents/main.py:634`, `src/agents/runtime/base_agent.py:117`. Tools are filtered only by name, never by schema/description/target. Paired with the SSRF above, this is the blast-radius amplifier for prompt injection: a poisoned tool can steer the model into exfiltrating conversation/file data to an attacker URL. Fix: enforce the existing `_TOOL_SERVER_OVERRIDES` map (pin expected server per tool) instead of only labeling.

### Medium

- MCP gateway runs `privileged: true` (dind) — `src/docker-compose-denis-mcp.yaml`. Digest-pinning of the active server images + catalog trust-boundary docs are done (`src/mcp_gateway/README.md`). Open residual is architectural: `privileged` is required by the dind variant (Swarm strips its caps), and the dind image can't terminate TLS, so `agents → mcp_gateway` stays plaintext on the overlay (host-allowlisted + trust header). Revisit with a TLS sidecar / mTLS or rootless-dind.
